<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Nassau-Dillenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1255 CE – 1739 CE (approx.) · capital Dillenburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ottonian Nassau county in the Siegerland and Dill valley, birthplace of William the Silent and the root of the Dutch royal house of Orange-Nassau.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1255 the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau">Nassau</a> divided their lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walram-ii-count-of-nassau">Walram</a> took the south, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-brunswick-gottingen">Otto I</a> received the northern lands with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herborn">Herborn</a>, founding the Ottonian line. His descendants ruled these lands as counts of Nassau-Dillenburg, repeatedly dividing and reuniting them.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was hilly and wooded, rich in iron ore from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegerland">Siegerland</a>. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-i-of-nassau">Engelbert I</a> acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> through marriage in 1403, linking the family to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-count-of-nassau-siegen">William the Rich</a> introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> in 1533, inherited <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-orange">Orange</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-count-of-nassau-dillenburg">John VI</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Reformed Protestantism</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herborn-academy">Herborn Academy</a> in 1584.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The lands were divided among several lines, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-nassau-siegen">Nassau-Siegen</a>, Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diez">Dietz</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-nassau-hadamar">Nassau-Hadamar</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> line died out in 1739, its lands passed to Nassau-Dietz, whose prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-prince-of-orange">William IV</a> became stadtholder of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> royal house.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1255 CE – 1739 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.68%;width:9.63%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1255 CE – 1739 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dillenburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Otto I of Nassau, William the Rich, John VI of Nassau-Dillenburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, then Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>